-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Parallel Ray:** If the ray direction is perpendicular to the plane normal (dot product is zero), the ray never intersects the plane. This is handled by the epsilon check [S2, unofficial].
-- **Precision:** Floating-point precision issues may arise when the ray is nearly parallel to the plane; using a small epsilon is necessary but the exact value is not specified [S2, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
+*   **Parallel Rays**: If the ray direction is perpendicular to the plane normal, the denominator is zero. This must be checked to avoid division by zero [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Back-face Culling**: If the renderer supports back-face culling, check if $D < 0$ (assuming outward-facing normals) and reject if so. Not explicitly mentioned in sources, but common in practice [G].
+*   **Precision**: For rays nearly parallel to the plane, $t$ can become very large, leading to precision issues in the intersection point calculation [G].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
+### Bounded Plane Intersection
+Intersects a ray with a finite plane segment (e.g., a rectangle). Requires additional tests to ensure the intersection point lies within the bounds. Choose when you need to limit the plane's extent, such as for a floor tile. See [Axis-Aligned Rectangle Hit Test](5279-axis-aligned-rectangle-hit-test.md).
