 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ constant time, involving a few dot products and a division [G].
-**Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ constant space [G].
-**Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources [G].
-**Strengths:** Not given in the sources [G].
-**Limitations:** Not given in the sources [G].
-**When to Use:** Not given in the sources [G].
-**When Not to Use:** Not given in the sources [G].
+*   **Time Complexity**: $O(1)$ constant time. Only a few dot products and a division are required.
+*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(1)$ constant space. No additional data structures are needed.
+*   **Strengths**: Extremely fast, simple to implement, no preprocessing required.
+*   **Limitations**: Only works for infinite planes. Cannot handle bounded planes or complex shapes.
+*   **When to use**: Ideal for ground planes, sky domes, or any infinite surface in a path tracer.
+*   **When not to use**: When the plane has finite bounds or when the scene contains many planes that can be efficiently accelerated with a BVH.
 
 ## Background
-The method is used in path tracers and ray tracers to handle ground planes [S1, unofficial]. It is mentioned in the context of adding a sun, a uniform sky, and an infinite ground plane to a renderer [S1, unofficial]. The analytical solution for ray-plane intersection is a fundamental building block in computer graphics, often taught in introductory graphics courses [G].
+The analytical ray-plane intersection is a fundamental technique in computer graphics, dating back to the early days of ray tracing. It is widely used in path tracers and real-time renderers for its simplicity and speed. The method is often used in conjunction with other primitives to form complex scenes. The "Infinite Ground Plane" is a common pattern in offline renderers like the one described in [S1, unofficial], where it is kept separate from the BVH for efficiency.
 
